    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 879858 and 936138?\n# Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "Okay, so I need to add 879,858 and 936,138. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly. Hmm, let's see:\n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. \n\nStarting with the ones place: 8 (from 879,858) plus 8 (from 936,138). 8 + 8 is 16. I'll write down 6 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place: 5 + 3 is 8, plus the carried-over 1 makes 9. So, 9 in the tens place. No carry-over here.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 8 + 1 is 9. So, 9 in the hundreds place. Still no carry-over.\n\nNow the thousands place: 9 (from the first number) plus 6 (from the second number) equals 15. Wait, so I write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 7 (from 879,858) plus 3 (from 936,138) is 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So, 1 in the ten-thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 8 + 9 is 17, plus the carried-over 1 gives 18. So, 8 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the millions place: There's only the carried-over 1, so that's 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,816,  let me check the digits again. Wait, maybe I messed up a place. Let me recount step by step.\n\nWait, let me write it out vertically:\n\n      879858\n    + 936138\n    -----------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8 + 8 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nTens: 5 + 3 = 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nHundreds: 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nThousands: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands: 7 + 3 = 10 + 1 = 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 + 9 = 17 + 1 = 18. Write 8, carry 1.\n\nMillions: 1 carried over. So total is 1,816, 000? Wait, let me add the numbers again.\n\nWait, 879,858 plus 936,138. Let me add them another way to verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking them into parts:\n\n879,858 + 900,000 = 1,779,858\n\nThen subtract the difference: 936,138 is 900k + 36,138. So adding 900k gives 1,779,858, then add 36,138.\n\n1,779,858 + 36,138:\n\n1,779,858 + 30,000 = 1,809,858\n\nThen +6,138: 1,809,858 + 6,000 = 1,815,858, then +138 = 1,816, 000? Wait, 1,815,858 + 138 is 1,816, 0 (since 858 + 138 = 996? Wait, no. Wait 858 + 138: 800+100=900, 58+38=96, so total 996. So 1,815,858 + 138 = 1,816, 0 (wait, 1,815,858 + 138 is 1,815,996). Hmm, so that would make the total 1,815,996?\n\nWait, now I'm confused. Maybe my first method was wrong. Let me do the addition step by step again carefully.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n879,858\n\n+936,138\n\nLet me write each digit:\n\nFirst number: 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nAdding each column:\n\nOnes: 8 +8 =16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nTens:5 +3 =8 +1=9. Write 9.\n\nHundreds:8 +1=9. Write 9.\n\nThousands:9 +6=15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:7 +3=10 +1=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +9=17 +1=18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 1. \n\nSo putting all together: 1 (million), 8 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 6 (ones). So that's 1,816, 996? Wait, no. Wait, the digits are:\n\nMillions place: 1\n\nHundred thousands: 8\n\nTen thousands: 1\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:6\n\nSo the number is 1,815,996?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\n1 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands) \u2192 800,000\n\n1 (ten thousands) \u2192 10,000\n\n5 (thousands) \u2192 5,000\n\n9 (hundreds) \u2192 900\n\n9 (tens) \u2192 90\n\n6 (ones) \u21926\n\nAdding those: 1,000,000 + 800,000 = 1,800,000\n\n+10,000 \u2192 1,810,000\n\n+5,000 \u2192 1,815,000\n\n+900 \u2192 1,815,900\n\n+90 \u2192 1,815,990\n\n+6 \u2192 1,815,996.\n\nAh, so the total is 1,815,996. But in my first calculation when I did the alternative method, I thought it was 1,816,000 but that was a miscalculation. So the correct answer is 1,815,996.\n\nWait, but in the first step-by-step addition, the digits were:\n\nAfter adding each column:\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:6\n\nSo that's 1,815,996. So that's correct. So where did I go wrong in the alternative method?\n\nLet me recalculate the alternative approach:\n\n879,858 + 936,138.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n879,858 + 900,000 = 1,779,858\n\nThen adding the remaining 36,138 (since 936,138 - 900,000 = 36,138):\n\n1,779,858 + 36,138.\n\nLet's compute that:\n\n1,779,858 + 30,000 = 1,809,858\n\nThen add 6,138:\n\n1,809,858 + 6,000 = 1,815,858\n\nThen add 138: 1,815,858 + 138 = 1,815,996. Yes, that matches. So my initial confusion was a miscalculation when I thought it was 1,816,000, but actually it's 1,815,996.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 1,815,996. So in the reasoning steps, I need to make sure each digit is correctly added with the carry-over. Let me check again the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8+8=16 \u2192 6, carryover 1.\n\nTens:5+3=8 +1=9 \u2192 9, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:8+1=9 \u21929, no carryover.\n\nThousands:9 (from first number's thousands place) +6 (second number's thousands place) =15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:7 +3=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8+9=17 +1=18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the digits are indeed 1,815,996. So the answer is correct. I think I just need to present this step-by-step in the template provided.\n<\uff5c/Assistant\uff5c>",
