maybe this method is confusing. Let me try adding each column again step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the hundreds place earlier. Let me redo the column addition carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\n\u2003297,173\n+370,973\n----------\n\nOnes: 3 +3 =6 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens:7+7=14, write 4, carry 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds:1 +9 =10, plus the 1 carried is 11. So write 1, carry 1. So hundreds digit is 1, carryover 1. \n\nThousands: 7 (from first number) +0 (from second) =7, plus the 1 carried is 8. So thousands digit is 8. \n\nTen-thousands: 9 (from first) +7 (from second) =16. So write 6, carryover 1. \n\nHundred-thousands: 2 +3 =5, plus the 1 carried is 6. \n\nSo putting all digits together: 6 (hundred-thousands), 6 (ten-thousands), 8 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (ones). So 668,146. \n\nWait, but when I broke it down earlier, adding the hundreds place as 100 +900 =1000, which would add an extra thousand. But in the column method, the hundreds place only contributed 1 with a carryover. Let me see:\n\nThe hundreds place in the first number is 1 (as in 100), and the second is 9 (as in 900). So 100 +900 =1000. That's 1 thousand. So when adding, the hundreds column's total is 10 (from 1+9) plus the carried 1 makes 11. So that 11 in the hundreds place is actually 11 hundreds, which is 1,100. So that's 1,000 + 100. So the 1,000 would carry over to the thousands place. Hence, the hundreds digit becomes 1 (from the remaining 100), and the 1,000 is added to the thousands place. \n\nSo the thousands place was originally 7 (from first number's thousands digit) plus 0 (second number's thousands digit) =7, plus the carried 1 makes 8. So that's correct. \n\nTherefore, the total is indeed 668,146. Let me confirm with another method: adding the two numbers as whole numbers.\n\n297,173 + 370,973. Let's add 297,173 + 300,000 = 597,173. Then add the remaining 70,973. \n\n597,173 +70,000 =667,173. Then add 973: 667,173 +973 = 668,146. \n\nYes, that matches. So the answer should be 668,146.\n",
