    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 972,672 and 331,315. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. \n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values:\n\n```\n  972672\n+ 331315\n---------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The ones digits are 2 and 5. Adding those gives 2 + 5 = 7. So the ones place of the sum is 7.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The digits here are 7 and 1. 7 + 1 equals 8. So the tens place is 8.\n\nNow the hundreds place. The digits are 6 and 3. Adding those gives 6 + 3 = 9. So the hundreds place is 9.\n\nMoving to the thousands place. Here we have 2 and 1. 2 + 1 is 3. So the thousands place is 3.\n\nNext up is the ten thousands place. The digits are 7 and 3. Adding them gives 7 + 3 = 10. Hmm, that's a two-digit number. I need to write down 0 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 to the next higher place (the hundred thousands place).\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The original digits here are 9 and 3. But we have a carryover of 1 from the previous step. So adding them together: 9 + 3 + 1 (carryover) = 13. So we put 3 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits to add, that 1 becomes the millions place digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,303,987. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake with the carryover.\n\nWait, let me recount each step carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes place: 2 + 5 = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 7 + 1 = 8 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 6 + 3 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 2 + 1 = 3 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen thousands: 7 (from 972,672) and 3 (from 331,315). 7+3=10. So write 0, carryover 1. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 3 = 12, plus the carryover 1 makes 13. So 3 in that place, carryover 1. Then the millions place is 1. So combining all digits: 1 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 0 (ten thousand), 3 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 8 (ten), 7 (one). So 1,303,987. That seems right.\n\nWait, but let me add them another way to confirm. Maybe breaking down the numbers:\n\n972,672 + 331,315.\n\nLet's split into parts:\n\n900,000 + 300,000 = 1,200,000\n\n70,000 + 30,000 = 100,000 \u2192 total so far 1,300,000\n\n2,000 + 1,000 = 3,000 \u2192 1,303,000\n\n600 + 300 = 900 \u2192 1,303,900\n\n70 + 10 = 80 \u2192 1,303,980\n\n2 +5 =7 \u2192 total 1,303,987. Yep, same result. Okay, that checks out. So the answer should be 1,303,987.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
