    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 994,737 and 136,202. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. \n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The first number is 994,737 and the second is 136,202. Let me write them vertically:\n\n```\n  994737\n+ 136202\n---------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The ones digits are 7 and 2. Adding those gives 7 + 2 = 9. So the ones place of the sum is 9.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The digits here are 3 and 0. 3 + 0 = 3. So the tens place is 3.\n\nNow the hundreds place. The digits are 7 and 2. 7 + 2 = 9. Hundreds place is 9.\n\nMoving to the thousands place. Here we have 4 and 6. 4 + 6 = 10. Hmm, that's a two-digit number. I need to write down 0 and carry over 1 to the next higher place.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The digits are 9 and 3, plus the carried over 1. So 9 + 3 + 1 = 13. Again, two digits. Write down 3 and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The digits are 9 and 1, plus the carried over 1. 9 + 1 + 1 = 11. So that's 1 in the hundred thousands place and another 1 carried over to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right: 9 (ones), 3 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), and 1 (millions). So the total is 1,130,939.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I made a mistake with the carrying over. Let me verify each step:\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes: 7 + 2 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 3 + 0 = 3 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 7 + 2 = 9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 4 + 6 = 10. Write 0, carry 1. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen thousands: 9 (from first number) + 3 (from second) + 1 (carry) = 13. So 3 with a carryover of 1. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred thousands: 9 + 1 + 1 = 11. So 1 and carryover 1. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nMillions place: The carryover 1. So the digits are 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands?), Wait, maybe I messed up the place values here. Let me write it out step by step numerically.\n\nLet me add them digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 9 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, the second number is 136,202. Breaking it down:\n\n1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 2 (ones). Yes.\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 7 + 2 = 9\n\nTens: 3 + 0 = 3\n\nHundreds: 7 + 2 = 9\n\nThousands: 4 + 6 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 9 (from first number) + 3 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 13. So 3 in the ten thousands place, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 9 (first number) + 1 (second number) + 1 (carryover) = 11. So 1 in the hundred thousands place, and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nMillions place: The carryover 1. So the millions digit is 1.\n\nPutting all together: 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (ones). So that's 1,130,939. \n\nHmm, that seems correct. Let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\n994,737 + 100,000 = 1,094,737\n\nThen add 36,202: 1,094,737 + 36,202 = ?\n\n1,094,737 + 30,000 = 1,124,737\n\n1,124,737 + 6,202 = 1,130,939. \n\nYes, same result. So the answer should be 1,130,939.\n",
