    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 986,066 and 207,829. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 6 in the ones place, and the second number also has a 9 there. Adding those together: 6 + 9 equals 15. Oh right, I need to carry over the 1 to the tens place. So I'll write down 5 in the ones place and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 6, the second has a 2. Adding those plus the carried-over 1: 6 + 2 + 1 = 9. So the tens place is 9. No carry-over here.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 0, and the second has an 8. 0 + 8 is 8. So that's straightforward. Hundreds place is 8.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 7. 6 + 7 is 13. Wait, so I write down 3 and carry over 1 again. \n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 0. Adding the carried-over 1: 8 + 0 + 1 = 9. So ten thousands place is 9.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 2. 9 + 2 equals 11. So that's 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over another 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried-over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place. \n\nPutting it all together: 1 (million), 1 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 3 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 9 (ten), 5 (one). So that's 1,193,895. Let me double-check to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. \n\nWait, let me add them again just to confirm. 986,066 plus 207,829. Let's break it down:\n\n986,066 + 200,000 = 1,186,066\n\nThen add 7,829: 1,186,066 + 7,000 = 1,193,066\n\nThen 829 more: 1,193,066 + 829 = 1,193,895. Yep, that matches. Okay, so the answer should be 1,193,895.\n",
