    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 452,332 and 751,820. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 2 in the ones place, and the second has a 0. Adding those gives 2 + 0 = 2. So the ones digit of the sum is 2.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 2. 3 + 2 is 5. So the tens digit is 5.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 3, and the second has an 8. 3 + 8 equals 11. Oh, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 1 in the hundreds place and carry over 1 to the thousands place.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 2, and the second has a 1. But don't forget the carried over 1. So 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. So the thousands digit is 4.\n\nNext is the ten-thousands place. The first number has a 5, and the second has a 5. 5 + 5 is 10. Again, that's a two-digit number. So I'll write 0 in the ten-thousands place and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The first number has a 4, and the second has a 7. Adding those gives 4 + 7 = 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So that's 2 in the hundred-thousands place and a carryover of 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes the millions digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,204,152. Wait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me add them another way. Maybe breaking them into parts. 452,332 + 700,000 is 1,152,332. Then add 51,820. 1,152,332 + 50,000 = 1,202,332. Then add 1,820: 1,202,332 + 1,820 = 1,204,152. Yeah, that matches. Okay, I think that's right.\n",
