    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 991,841 and 139,758. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 1 in the ones place, and the second number has an 8 there. Adding those together: 1 + 8 equals 9. So the ones place of the sum is 9. That part was straightforward.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The first number has a 4, and the second has a 5. 4 + 5 is 9. So the tens place is also 9. Still no carrying over here.\n\nNow the hundreds place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 7. 8 + 7 is 15. Oh, wait, that's more than 10. So I need to write down 5 in the hundreds place and carry over 1 to the thousands place. Let me note that down: carryover of 1.\n\nMoving on to the thousands place. The first number has a 1, and the second has a 9. Adding those gives 1 + 9 = 10, plus the carried over 1 makes it 11. So I write down 1 in the thousands place and carry over another 1 to the next higher place. So now there's another carryover of 1.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 3. 9 + 3 is 12, plus the carried over 1 equals 13. So I put 3 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 1. Adding those gives 9 + 1 = 10, plus the carried over 1 makes it 11. So that's 1 in the hundred thousands place and another 1 carried over to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried over 1 becomes the millions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together: 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (ones). Wait, let me check that again. Wait, the thousands place was 11, so after carrying over, the thousands digit is 1, and the ten thousands was 13, so 3 there. Then hundred thousands place was 9 +1 +1? Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me recount step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out vertically to be precise. Let me try again:\n\n      991,841\n    + 139,758\n    -----------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 1 + 8 = 9. Good.\n\nTens: 4 +5 =9. Still okay.\n\nHundreds: 8 +7 =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 1 (from first number) +9 (from second) =10, plus the 1 carried over is 11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 9 (from first number) +3 (from second) =12, plus the carried 1 is 13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 9 (from first) +1 (from second) =10, plus the carried 1 is 11. So write 1 and carryover 1.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carried over 1 becomes the millions place. So the total is 1,131,599?\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts. Let's break them down:\n\n991,841 is 900,000 + 90,000 + 1,000 + 800 + 40 +1\n\n139,758 is 100,000 + 30,000 + 9,000 + 700 +50 +8\n\nAdding each part:\n\nMillions: 900k + 100k = 1,000,000\n\nHundred thousands: 90k +30k =120k\n\nTen thousands: 1k (Wait, no, maybe I need to reorganize properly. Let me see:\n\nWait, perhaps better to add the numbers step by step:\n\nFirst, 991,841 + 100,000 = 1,091,841\n\nThen add 39,758 (since 139,758 -100,000 =39,758)\n\n1,091,841 + 39,758:\n\nAdding 30,000: 1,121,841\n\nThen 9,000: 1,130,841\n\nThen 700: 1,131,541\n\nThen 50: 1,131,591\n\nThen 8: 1,131,599. Okay, same result. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using calculator-like addition:\n\n991,841\n\n+139,758\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1+8=9 (ones)\n\n4+5=9 (tens)\n\n8+7=15 (hundreds, write 5, carry 1)\n\n1 (thousands) +9 (thousands) +1 carried =11 (write 1, carry 1)\n\n9 (ten thousands) +3 (ten thousands) +1 carried =13 (write 3, carry1)\n\n9 (hundred thousands) +1 (hundred thousands) +1 carried =11 (write 1, carry1)\n\nFinally, the carried 1 in millions place.\n\nSo total is 1,131,599. That seems consistent. So the answer should be 1,131,599.\n",
