    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 205,954 and 380,521. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. \n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. Let me add the ones digits first. 4 plus 1 is 5. That's straightforward. \n\nNext, the tens place. The digits there are 5 and 2. 5 plus 2 equals 7. So far, so good. \n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The digits are 9 and 5. Hmm, 9 plus 5 is 14. Wait, I need to write down 4 and carry over 1 to the next column. \n\nNow the thousands place. The digits here are 5 and 0, but don't forget the carried-over 1. So 5 + 0 + 1 is 6. \n\nNext up is the ten-thousands place. The first number has a 0 and the second has an 8. Adding those gives 8. \n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The first number has a 2 and the second has a 3. 2 plus 3 is 5. \n\nPutting all those together, the sum should be 586,475. Let me double-check each column to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nOnes: 4+1=5 \u2714\ufe0f. Tens:5+2=7 \u2714\ufe0f. Hundreds:9+5=14, carry 1 \u2714\ufe0f. Thousands:5+0+1=6 \u2714\ufe0f. Ten-thousands:0+8=8 \u2714\ufe0f. Hundred-thousands:2+3=5 \u2714\ufe0f. Yep, looks right. So the answer is 586,475.\n",
