    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 636,547 and 776,633. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are lined up correctly. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 7 in the ones place, and the second has a 3. Adding those together: 7 + 3 equals 10. Wait, that's 10. So I write down 0 in the ones place and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nMoving to the tens place next. The first number has a 4, and the second has a 3. Adding those gives 4 + 3 = 7, but don't forget the carried over 1. So 7 + 1 = 8. So the tens place is 8. No carryover here.\n\nNext is the hundreds place. The first number has a 5, and the second has a 6. 5 + 6 is 11. So I write down 1 in the hundreds place and carry over another 1 to the thousands place.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 6. 6 + 6 = 12, plus the carried over 1 makes 13. So I put 3 in the thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nMoving to the ten thousands place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 7. 3 + 7 = 10, plus the carried over 1 is 11. So write 1 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 7. 6 + 7 = 13, plus the carried over 1 makes 14. So that's 4 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,413,180. Let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in the carrying over. Starting from the right again: 7+3=10, carry 1. Then 4+3+1=8. 5+6=11, carry 1. 6+6+1=13, carry 1. 3+7+1=11, carry 1. 6+7+1=14, carry 1. Then the 1 in the millions. Yeah, that seems right. So the final answer should be 1,413,180.\n",
