    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 426,370 and 558,405. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let's see:\n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. \n\nStarting with the ones place: 0 + 5. That should be 5. Easy enough. \n\nNext, the tens place: 7 + 0. That's 7. \n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 3 + 4. Wait, 3 (from 426,370) and 4 (from 558,405). That adds up to 7. \n\nNow the thousands place: 6 + 8. 6 plus 8 is 14. Oh, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 4 and carry over 1 to the next column. \n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The original numbers have 2 and 5 here. But don't forget the carried over 1. So 2 + 5 is 7, plus 1 makes 8. \n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place: 4 and 5. 4 + 5 is 9. \n\nPutting all those together: 9 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (ones). So that should be 984,775. \n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me add them another way. Maybe breaking them into parts. \n\n426,370 + 558,405. Let's split into 400,000 + 26,370 and 500,000 + 58,405. Adding the big parts first: 400k + 500k = 900,000. Then 26,370 + 58,405. \n\n26,370 + 58,405: 26k +58k is 84k, and 370 +405 is 775. So total is 84,775. Adding to the 900k gives 984,775. Yep, same result. Okay, that seems right.\n",
