    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 895,991 and 448,372. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 1 in the ones place, and the second has a 2. Adding those gives 1 + 2 = 3. So the ones digit of the sum is 3. \n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 9 and the second has a 7. 9 + 7 is 16. I'll write down 6 and carry over 1 to the hundreds place. \n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 9, the second has a 3. Adding those gives 12, plus the carried-over 1 makes 13. So I write 3 here and carry over 1 again. \n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 5 and the second has an 8. 5 + 8 is 13, plus the carried 1 equals 14. So I put 4 in the thousands place and carry over 1. \n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 9 and the second has a 4. 9 + 4 is 13, plus the carried 1 makes 14. So I write 4 here and carry over 1. \n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has an 8 and the second has a 4. Adding those gives 12, plus the carried 1 equals 13. So that's 3 in the hundred thousands place and 1 carried over to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes the millions digit. \n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,344,363. Wait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me add them another way. Maybe breaking them into parts. \n\n895,991 is 800,000 + 90,000 + 5,000 + 900 + 90 + 1. \n448,372 is 400,000 + 40,000 + 8,000 + 300 + 70 + 2. \n\nAdding the hundreds of thousands: 800k + 400k = 1,200,000. \nThen 90k + 40k = 130,000. \n5k +8k =13k. \n900 +300=1,200. \n90+70=160. \n1+2=3. \n\nAdding all those: 1,200,000 +130,000 =1,330,000. Then +13,000 is 1,343,000. Then +1,200 is 1,344,200. Then +160 is 1,344,360. Plus 3 gives 1,344,363. Okay, that matches. So the answer should be correct.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
