    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 276,312 and 788,554. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. \n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. Let me add the ones digits first. The ones digit of 276,312 is 2, and the ones digit of 788,554 is 4. Adding those together: 2 + 4 equals 6. So I write down 6 in the ones place.\n\nNext, the tens place. The tens digit of 276,312 is 1, and the tens digit of 788,554 is 5. 1 + 5 is 6. So that's 6 in the tens place.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The hundreds digit in the first number is 3, and in the second number, it's 5. 3 + 5 equals 8. So the hundreds place is 8.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has 6 in the thousands place, and the second has 8. 6 + 8 is 14. Hmm, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 4 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has 7 here, and the second has 8. Adding those gives 15, plus the carried over 1 makes 16. So I write 6 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has 2, and the second has 7. 2 + 7 is 9, plus the carried over 1 makes 10. So I write down 0 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,064,866. Let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any mistakes with the carrying. Starting from the right again: 2+4=6, 1+5=6, 3+5=8, 6+8=14 (carry 1), 7+8+1=16 (carry 1), 2+7+1=10. Yep, that looks right. So the final answer should be 1,064,866.\n",
