    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 550,091 and 323,377. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let's see:\n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. Let me add each column starting from the right.\n\nStarting with the ones place: 1 + 7. That should be 8. Easy enough.\n\nNext, the tens place: 9 + 7. Wait, 9 plus 7 is 16. Oh right, so I write down 6 and carry over 1 to the hundreds place.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 0 (from the first number) plus 3 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 1. That adds up to 4. So, 0 + 3 is 3, plus 1 is 4. No carry-over here.\n\nNow the thousands place: 0 (from the first number) plus 3 (from the second number). That's straightforward, 3. Still no carry-over.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place: 5 (from the first number) plus 2 (from the second number). 5 + 2 is 7. So that's 7 with nothing to carry over.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place: 5 (from the first number) plus 3 (from the second number). 5 + 3 equals 8. \n\nPutting all those digits together, the sum should be 873,468. Let me double-check to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Wait, let me add them again just to be sure. \n\n550,091  \n+323,377  \n=?\n\nAdding the ones: 1+7=8  \nTens: 9+7=16 (carry 1)  \nHundreds: 0+3+1=4  \nThousands: 0+3=3  \nTen thousands: 5+2=7  \nHundred thousands:5+3=8  \nYes, that gives 873,468. I think that's correct. Maybe I should use another method to verify. Let's see, breaking the numbers into parts:\n\n550,091 can be 550,000 + 91  \n323,377 can be 323,000 + 377  \nAdding the larger parts first: 550,000 + 323,000 = 873,000  \nThen the smaller parts: 91 + 377 = 468  \nAdding those together: 873,000 + 468 = 873,468. \n\nYep, same result. Okay, that seems right. I think the answer is 873,468.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
