    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 988,285 and 707,132. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 5 in the ones place, and the second has a 2. Adding those together: 5 + 2 equals 7. So the ones place of the sum is 7. That part was straightforward.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 3. 8 plus 3 is 11. Oh, wait, that's more than 10. So I need to carry over 1 to the hundreds place. I'll write down 1 in the tens place and remember to add the carried-over 1 later. \n\nNow the hundreds place. The first number has a 2, and the second has a 1. Adding those gives 2 + 1 = 3, plus the carried-over 1 makes 4. So the hundreds digit is 4. No carry-over here.\n\nMoving to the thousands place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 7. 8 + 7 is 15. Again, that's more than 10. So I write down 5 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has an 8, and the second has a 0. 8 + 0 is 8, plus the carried-over 1 makes 9. So the ten thousands digit is 9. No carry-over this time.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 7. Adding those gives 9 + 7 = 16. So we write down 6 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that carried-over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place. \n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,695,417. Let me double-check each step to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nOnes: 5+2=7 \u2714\ufe0f. Tens: 8+3=11, so 1 with carryover \u2714\ufe0f. Hundreds: 2+1+1=4 \u2714\ufe0f. Thousands: 8+7=15, so 5 with carryover \u2714\ufe0f. Ten thousands: 8+0+1=9 \u2714\ufe0f. Hundred thousands: 9+7=16, so 6 with carryover \u2714\ufe0f. Millions: 1 \u2714\ufe0f. Yeah, that seems right. I think that's the correct answer.\n",
