    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 362,768 and 547,482. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has an 8 in the ones place, and the second has a 2. Adding those together: 8 + 2 equals 10. Wait, that's 10, so I write down 0 and carry over 1 to the tens place. \n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 6, and the second has an 8. Adding those plus the carried-over 1: 6 + 8 is 14, plus 1 makes 15. So I write 5 in the tens place and carry over 1 again.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 7 and the second has a 4. 7 + 4 is 11, plus the carried 1 makes 12. So I put 2 in the hundreds place and carry over 1.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 2 and the second has a 7. 2 + 7 is 9, plus the 1 carried over gives 10. So I write 0 here and carry over 1 again.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 6 and the second has a 4. 6 + 4 is 10, plus the carried 1 makes 11. So I write 1 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 to the next.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 3 and the second has a 5. 3 + 5 is 8, plus the carried 1 equals 9. So that's the last digit.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 910,250. Let me check again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in the carrying over. Starting from the right: 8+2=10, carry 1. Then 6+8+1=15, carry 1. 7+4+1=12, carry 1. 2+7+1=10, carry 1. 6+4+1=11, carry 1. 3+5+1=9. Yeah, that seems right. So the answer is 910,250.\n",
