    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 194,793 and 947,190. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting with the ones place: the first number has a 3 in the ones, and the second has a 0. Adding those gives 3. That's straightforward. \n\nNext, the tens place: 9 and 9. 9 plus 9 is 18. Oh, wait, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 8 in the tens place and carry over 1 to the hundreds. \n\nMoving to the hundreds place: the first number has 7, the second has 1. Adding those plus the carried-over 1 gives 7 + 1 + 1 = 9. So the hundreds digit is 9. No carry-over here. \n\nNow the thousands place: 4 and 7. 4 + 7 is 11. Again, that's over 10, so I'll put 1 in the thousands place and carry over 1. \n\nTens of thousands place: 9 and 4. Adding them plus the carried-over 1: 9 + 4 = 13, plus 1 is 14. So I write 4 here and carry over 1 again. \n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place: 1 and 9. 1 + 9 is 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So that's 1 in the hundred thousands place and another 1 carried over to the millions. \n\nWait, but the first number only has six digits and the second has six as well. Adding them might result in a seven-digit number. Let me check again. \n\nBreaking it down step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 3 + 0 = 3  \nTens: 9 + 9 = 18 (write 8, carry 1)  \nHundreds: 7 + 1 + 1 (carry) = 9  \nThousands: 4 + 7 = 11 (write 1, carry 1)  \nTen-thousands: 9 + 4 + 1 (carry) = 14 (write 4, carry 1)  \nHundred-thousands: 1 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 11 (write 1, carry 1)  \nMillions place: the carryover 1. \n\nSo putting it all together, the sum should be 1,141,983. Let me verify with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers as whole:\n\n194,793 + 947,190. Let's see:\n\n194,793 + 900,000 = 1,094,793  \nThen add 47,190: 1,094,793 + 47,190 = 1,141,983. \n\nYes, that matches. So the final answer is correct.\n",
