    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 147,974 and 967,977. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 4 in the ones place, and the second has a 7. Adding those gives 4 + 7 = 11. Oh, right, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over 1 to the tens place. \n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 7, and the second also has a 7. Adding those plus the carried-over 1 gives 7 + 7 + 1 = 15. Again, that's 5 in the tens place and carry over 1 to the hundreds.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 9 and the second has a 9. Adding them plus the carried 1: 9 + 9 + 1 = 19. So, 9 in hundreds, carry over 1 again.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 7 and the second has a 7. 7 + 7 + 1 (carry) = 15. So 5 in thousands, carry over 1.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 4 and the second has a 6. 4 + 6 + 1 = 11. So 1 in ten thousands, carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 1 and the second has a 9. 1 + 9 + 1 = 11. So that's 1 in the hundred thousands and carry over 1 to the millions place. Wait, but there's no higher place, so that 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,115,951. Let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes: 4 +7=11, carry 1. Tens:7+7+1=15, carry1. Hundreds:9+9+1=19, carry1. Thousands:7+7+1=15, carry1. Ten thousands:4+6+1=11, carry1. Hundred thousands:1+9+1=11. So yes, that gives 1,115,951. I think that's correct.\n",
