    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 266,665 and 880,147. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. \n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. Let me add the ones digits first. 5 plus 7 is 12. Hmm, I need to write down the 2 and carry over the 1 to the tens place. \n\nNext, the tens place. The digits there are 6 and 4. Adding those gives 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So I write down 1 and carry over another 1. \n\nMoving to the hundreds place. Both numbers have 6 and 1. 6 plus 1 is 7, plus the carried 1 equals 8. No carry-over here. \n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has 6 and the second has 0. 6 plus 0 is 6. Adding any carried-over digits? No, so just 6. \n\nNext up is the ten-thousands place. The first number has 6 and the second has 8. 6 plus 8 is 14. Wait, so I write down 4 and carry over 1. \n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place. The first number has 2 and the second has 8. 2 plus 8 is 10, plus the carried-over 1 makes 11. So that's 1 in the hundred-thousands place and another 1 carried over to the millions place. Since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes the millions digit. \n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 1,146,812. Let me double-check each step to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Ones place: 5+7=12, correct. Tens: 6+4+1=11, right. Hundreds: 6+1+1=8. Thousands: 6+0=6. Ten-thousands: 6+8=14. Hundred-thousands: 2+8+1=11. Yeah, that adds up. So the final answer should be 1,146,812.\n",
