    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 104,203 and 299,060. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let me make sure the places are correct. \n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number has a 3 in the ones place, and the second has a 0. Adding those gives 3 + 0 = 3. So the ones digit of the sum is 3.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 0, and the second has a 6. 0 + 6 is 6. So the tens digit is 6.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 2, and the second has a 0. 2 + 0 = 2. So the hundreds digit is 2.\n\nNow the thousands place. The first number has a 4, and the second has a 9. 4 + 9 = 13. Wait, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 3 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. The first number has a 0, and the second has a 9. Adding the carried over 1 gives 0 + 9 + 1 = 10. So I write down 0 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place. The first number has a 1, and the second has a 2. Adding the carried over 1 gives 1 + 2 + 1 = 4. So the hundred thousands digit is 4.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum should be 403,263. Let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any mistakes with the carrying. Starting over:\n\n104,203  \n+299,060  \n=  \nOnes: 3+0=3  \nTens: 0+6=6  \nHundreds: 2+0=2  \nThousands: 4+9=13 (carry 1)  \nTen thousands: 0+9+1=10 (carry 1)  \nHundred thousands: 1+2+1=4  \nYes, that seems right. So the total is 403,263.\n",
