    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "Okay, so I need to add 360,273 and 497,252. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Hmm, let's see:\n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. Let me add each column starting from the right.\n\nStarting with the ones place: 3 (from 360,273) and 2 (from 497,252). 3 + 2 is 5. So the ones digit of the sum is 5.\n\nNext, the tens place: 7 and 5. 7 + 5 equals 12. Oh, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 2 in the tens place and carry over 1 to the hundreds column.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 2 (from the first number) plus 2 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 1. That's 2 + 2 + 1 = 5. So the hundreds digit is 5.\n\nNow the thousands place: 0 (from 360,273) and 7 (from 497,252). Adding those gives 0 + 7 = 7. No carry-over here, so the thousands digit is 7.\n\nNext up is the ten thousands place. The first number has 6 and the second has 9. 6 + 9 is 15. Wait, that's 15. So I write down 5 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: 3 (from 360,273) and 4 (from 497,252) plus the carried-over 1. That's 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. So the hundred thousands digit is 8.\n\nPutting all those digits together, the sum should be 857,525. Let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any mistakes in carrying over or adding each column. Let me add them again quickly:\n\n360,273\n+497,252\n=?\nAdding ones: 3+2=5\nTens:7+5=12 (carry 1)\nHundreds:2+2+1=5\nThousands:0+7=7\nTen thousands:6+9=15 (carry 1)\nHundred thousands:3+4+1=8\nYes, that seems right. So the total is 857,525.\n",
